  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...\nIssued 213 PM MDT FRI OCT 23 2026\nA compact shortwave upper trough will approach the Pacific NW on \nMonday and bring a return closer to normal temperatures, along with \nanother round of precipitation to the region Monday evening into \nTuesday. There are still some timing differences amongst \ndeterministic model guidance - most notably the ECMWF which is \nfaster and depicts a secondary upstream system off the BC coast - \nbut probabilistic guidance favors Tuesday as the more active day, \nespecially into west-central and central Idaho. QPF amounts are \ngenerally expected to remain near or below 0.25 inch even in the \nmountains, given the lack of a significant moisture tap. Snow levels \nwill remain above 6000 feet throughout this event as well, which \nwill limit snow accumulations primarily to higher elevation \nmountainous areas. Temperatures will modulate during the week and \nremain slightly above seasonal normals. In the wake of the early \nweek system, the pattern will remain unsettled, with low-end (up to \n20%) probabilities maintained each day, especially in the mountains. \nThe next significant storm system takes shape Thursday and Friday. \nMoisture will remain quite limited and remain confined to the Desert \nSouthwest, with precipitation chances beginning to increase again on \nFriday.",
